{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>By the highway.</b> At first they were telling them to come into their city, only they should be careful not to enter into the field and vineyard, in the manner of the camps of a great people that strip the threshing-floors, entering into vineyards; but let them go by the king's road, which is a public road and not a private road. And they were saying that they should not drink their water from their wells at all. Afterward he sent to them that they should not come near the cities at all, but go by the highway that ascends to the land of Israel, which is paved and open to all; and if they drink the water of the rivers on the road — they and their animals when they pass through a river — they will pay their price. Therefore they said: ״רק אין דבר״ — there is nothing in this. Thus wrote the Ramban, z\"l." "<b>With my foot.</b> The sense of רגלי is 'my legions,' as in (Song of Songs 7:2) רגליו. And from here you may learn that ״ועמדו רגליו על הר הזיתים״ (Zechariah 14:4), said of the Holy One, blessed be He, the intention in it is His legions, in order to remove from Him, blessed be He, all attributes of corporeality. And if one should say that it is not comparable — for the legions of the lower beings are fit to be called thus, since the foot is an instrument of walking and it is not the way of the lower ones to walk without feet; but concerning the upper legions, which have no instrument of walking and among whom the language of motion does not apply, how is it possible to call them by this name? And the answer to the matter is that this is a term shared in common: for in every thing, its uppermost part is called its 'head' and the lowest part of the thing is called its 'feet.' And since it has been established that the Creator, may He be exalted, is the head of all existent beings, it is possible to call the legions of all existent beings His 'feet.' Or רגליו may mean His causes, according to the opinion of the Rambam, z\"l, and this is like the rest of the names that are names of physical organs in man, which are said of the Creator, may He be exalted, such as (Exodus 17) ״פי ה׳״, (Deuteronomy 11) ״עיני ה׳״, (Numbers 11) ״אזני ה׳״. And I have already explained all these expressions in connection with the verse (Genesis 6:6) ״ויתעצב אל לבו״."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {1 "He cites Song of Songs 7:2 (רגליו) implicitly; Zechariah citation written זכריה י״ד:ד׳ normalized to Zechariah 14:4; Exodus 17, Deuteronomy 11, Numbers 11 cited by chapter only, normalized."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:34:27.890581635Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}